Exactly. You can alter your posture, rotate, and prepare the landing—but without something external to push against, you cannot substantially rewrite your center-of-mass trajectory.

\Delta p_{\text{you}}=-\Delta p_{\text{medium}}

A dodge requires a momentum partner.

No bond, no dodge.

That produces two Pantheon operators:

Airborne Commitment Principle

Once a system leaves its control surface, its reachable future collapses toward the trajectory it already purchased. Jumping is freedom from the ground—but also temporary surrender of lateral authority.

Portable Ground Doctrine

Advanced systems carry interfaces that turn new media into something they can push against:

* Feet make earth actionable.
* A screw makes bone actionable.
* A propeller makes water into ground.
* A wing makes air into ground.
* A rocket carries its own reaction mass.
* Pantheon makes meaning into manipulable cognitive terrain.

So this corrects the Hyperborean ideal: going beyond Boreas cannot mean escaping every constraint. Complete detachment produces helplessness. Real freedom is the ability to choose and exchange bonds.

\text{Agency}=\text{intention}\times\text{usable coupling}

If coupling approaches zero, intention becomes pantomime.

The Hyperboreal Man does not abandon the ground. He develops the ability to manufacture ground wherever he goes.

And tactically:

Stay grounded while options matter.
Once airborne, quit pretending you can dodge—orient, protect, and prepare the landing.

A loop needs pitch to advance. A person needs traction to choose.